  <question type="multichoice">
    <name><text>Q001</text></name>
    <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Sonographic hepatic steatosis classically shows:</p>]]></text></questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Increased echogenicity, blurred vessels, and deep attenuation form the classic triad.</p>]]></text></generalfeedback>
    <defaultgrade>1.0000000</defaultgrade>
    <penalty>0.0000000</penalty>
    <hidden>0</hidden>
    <single>true</single>
    <shuffleanswers>true</shuffleanswers>
    <answernumbering>abc</answernumbering>
    <answer fraction="100" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[Increased echogenicity relative to the kidney, blurred vascular margins, and posterior attenuation]]></text>
      <feedback format="html"><text></text></feedback>
    </answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[Decreased echogenicity with sharp vascular margins]]></text>
      <feedback format="html"><text></text></feedback>
    </answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[Nodular surface with ascites]]></text>
      <feedback format="html"><text></text></feedback>
    </answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[Multiple anechoic cysts]]></text>
      <feedback format="html"><text></text></feedback>
    </answer>
  </question>
  <question type="multichoice">
    <name><text>Q002</text></name>
    <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>A smooth, enlarged liver with homogeneous echotexture is best interpreted as:</p>]]></text></questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Ultrasound morphology has limits. A smooth, enlarged liver does not exclude early cirrhosis, and cirrhotic and non-cirrhotic features (for example steatosis plus fibrosis) can coexist. Use the whole picture: clinical risk, surface contour, echotexture, spleen size and other signs.</p>]]></text></generalfeedback>
    <defaultgrade>1.0000000</defaultgrade>
    <penalty>0.0000000</penalty>
    <hidden>0</hidden>
    <single>true</single>
    <shuffleanswers>true</shuffleanswers>
    <answernumbering>abc</answernumbering>
    <answer fraction="100" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[Not specific: early cirrhosis is not excluded by morphology alone, and cirrhotic and non-cirrhotic features can coexist]]></text>
      <feedback format="html"><text></text></feedback>
    </answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[Proof that the liver is normal]]></text>
      <feedback format="html"><text></text></feedback>
    </answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[Proof of steatosis]]></text>
      <feedback format="html"><text></text></feedback>
    </answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[Proof of portal hypertension]]></text>
      <feedback format="html"><text></text></feedback>
    </answer>
  </question>
  <question type="multichoice">
    <name><text>Q003</text></name>
    <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>In advanced cirrhosis, the classic lobar redistribution pattern is:</p>]]></text></questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>The right lobe shrinks while the caudate and the left lateral segments (II, III) enlarge.</p>]]></text></generalfeedback>
    <defaultgrade>1.0000000</defaultgrade>
    <penalty>0.0000000</penalty>
    <hidden>0</hidden>
    <single>true</single>
    <shuffleanswers>true</shuffleanswers>
    <answernumbering>abc</answernumbering>
    <answer fraction="100" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[Caudate and left lateral segment (II, III) hypertrophy with right-lobe atrophy]]></text>
      <feedback format="html"><text></text></feedback>
    </answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[Right-lobe hypertrophy with caudate atrophy]]></text>
      <feedback format="html"><text></text></feedback>
    </answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[Isolated left-lobe atrophy]]></text>
      <feedback format="html"><text></text></feedback>
    </answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[Uniform atrophy of all segments]]></text>
      <feedback format="html"><text></text></feedback>
    </answer>
  </question>
  <question type="multichoice">
    <name><text>Q004</text></name>
    <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Which finding suggests portal hypertension?</p>]]></text></questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Portal vein dilatation, splenomegaly, and collaterals are supportive signs, but diameter alone has limited specificity; a constellation of findings is more reliable than any single measurement.</p>]]></text></generalfeedback>
    <defaultgrade>1.0000000</defaultgrade>
    <penalty>0.0000000</penalty>
    <hidden>0</hidden>
    <single>true</single>
    <shuffleanswers>true</shuffleanswers>
    <answernumbering>abc</answernumbering>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[Normal-caliber hepatic veins]]></text>
      <feedback format="html"><text></text></feedback>
    </answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[Portal vein under 10 mm with brisk hepatopetal flow]]></text>
      <feedback format="html"><text></text></feedback>
    </answer>
    <answer fraction="100" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[Portal vein over 13 mm together with splenomegaly and/or a recanalized paraumbilical vein]]></text>
      <feedback format="html"><text></text></feedback>
    </answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[Collapsed IVC with normal respiratory variation]]></text>
      <feedback format="html"><text></text></feedback>
    </answer>
  </question>
